OpenMUI compatibility scope and checks
======================================

Scope
-----
OpenMUI targets AmigaOS 3.1 or newer, a 68000 and no FPU. The priority is
the behaviour of MUI 3.8 and 3.9 programs. Bundled classes are separate
libraries. Rawimage.mcc and Pixmap.mui provide the image classes used by
the built-in preferences pages. Not provided: muiscreen.library, AROS
module ports and MUI features that no known application needs.

Version policy
--------------
The library version is [VERSION], the release [RELEASE] ([STAGE]). The
library list reports MUI 5 (21.227) so that every program can open it;
each program is handed the MUI version it asks for: 19.35 (MUI 3.8),
20.6000 (MUI 4) or 21.227 (MUI 5). External MCCs report their own
versions. The release date identifies the OpenMUI release. Native
version definitions are in native_version.h. Building with MUI5_TEST=0
gives AROS version 19.81 and built-in class version 1.1.

MUI 4 and 5 additions
---------------------
Implemented: library vectors, layout and pointers, menu images and
shortcuts, String editing, Title tabs, newer List and Text interfaces,
Datamap, Objectmap, Textdata and Selectgroup. Data containers use pools
and binary-search lookup. Textdata borrows its source and owns its
converted buffers; codesets.library is optional. Without it, conversion
covers Latin-1, UTF-8 and big-endian UCS-2/UTF-16. Encoding conversion
alone does not add Unicode glyphs to the current Amiga font.

Also implemented: Dataspace Get/Count, directory byte totals through a
64-bit pointer, Group and Family reordering, TextColor, Relayout, Filepanel
and Fontpanel, separate-process MUI ASL requests, multi-file results, user
font families, Text marking and additional escapes, menu copying, List
column mapping and String hook actions, Process, Keyadjust and Fontdisplay.
Textdata_SourceEncoding matches the observed no-op behaviour of MUI
21.227 in the tested cases. Window opacity is stored; OS 3 has no
composited transparent windows.

As in MUI 5, String implements the Textinput interface: marks, clipboard
and edit methods, numeric input and the Textinput attributes.
Textinput.mcc and Textinputscroll.mcc 29 are thin wrappers. A multiline
String edits lines: Return inserts a newline and the cursor keys move
between lines. Textinputscroll shows no scrollbars.

Not implemented: Screenmodepanel, Slave, undocumented preferences
classes, complete theme compatibility. Native ASL handles unsupported
requester options. External MCC versions and AfA OS interaction need their
own application checks. Reproducible probes, reference differences and
validation limits: tests/native/README.mui5.md in the source.

Checks that execute linked code
-------------------------------
These run m68k code with modelled OS services ("make check"):
- Public library gates, register hooks and classic structure prefixes
- Built-in and external class ownership, failure cleanup and expunge
- Notification removal, re-entry, source disposal, symbolic destinations
- Typed configuration lookup, numeric defaults, custom data pointers
- Buffered input, ARexx fallback hooks, AppIcon drops, legacy menus, help
- Floattext wrapping, dynamic group relayout, explicit cycle ordering
- Numeric scale intervals, reverse mapping, large-range arithmetic
- Signed String selection ranges, explicit up/down focus targets
- Bitmap remap ownership, image allocation failure paths, nested clips
- Pixmap raw/RLE/bzip2 decoding, data replacement, failed-allocation cleanup

They are not an AmigaOS application compatibility matrix and not a
hardware result. When comparing MUI and OpenMUI, use separate OS
installations, in particular with third-party MCCs that open
muimaster.library themselves.

Rendering and lifetime limits
-----------------------------
Planar Bitmap rendering treats nonzero global alpha as opaque while
retaining its mask; zero alpha is transparent. Core RTG rendering
opens CyberGraphX V41 on demand and uses its classic pixel-array calls
for software alpha blending. Text RGB/ARGB and inline image pixels are
covered by a Picasso96 probe; complete RTG themes are not. Window refresh
follows
the configured preferences. Both refresh modes use clip regions outside
an update, and managed refresh preserves pending damage while changing
regions. Broad redraw/text caches need measurements and GUI evidence
before adoption.

Rawimage.mcc and Pixmap.mui are separate, demand-loaded libraries. Pixmap
accepts RGB24, ARGB32 and CLUT8 images with raw, RLE or bzip2 storage.
Planar screens use graphics.library bitmaps, acquire only the pens actually
used, and approximate fractional alpha with a 4x4 coverage mask. They do
not require CyberGraphX. Truecolor rendering requires CyberGraphX v41;
its classic read/write API supplies software alpha blending.

Bzip2 scratch space is bounded by the declared uncompressed image size,
using the small-memory decoder. A 24x19 ARGB image needs about 70 KiB at
peak during decoding, rather than a 900 KiB block's full scratch space.
Decoded data is retained for reuse; bitmap, mask and pen ownership follows
the screen setup/cleanup lifetime. Borrowed raw image data remains owned
by the caller. Truecolor rendering needs more testing.

Notification list delivery tolerates callbacks removing notifications or
disposing a plain Notify source. An arbitrary class method may still need
its own instance after sending a notification. Defer disposal of such
objects through the application's pushed-method queue until the callback
unwinds. Explicit notification destinations remain caller-owned: remove
notifications before destroying independently owned targets.

Diagnostic TRACE=1 builds use a bounded 64 KiB T:zune.log fallback, with
messages truncated to 511 bytes and files closed after each write. Shell
output remains caller-owned. Use TRACE=0 for performance measurements.

TheBar
------
TheBar 26.22, TheBarVirt, TheButton, TheBar.mcp and the Toolbar wrapper
are built for the 68000 without an FPU and loaded only when requested. The
five stripped modules total 184,200 bytes. SDK headers, catalogs,
documentation and Examples/TheBarDemo are included (Docs/thebar/Native).
On AmigaOS 3.2.3, TheBarDemo CHECK completes three construction,
attribute, window-open and disposal cycles; the preferences page renders
and Cancel exits with return code 0. Optional RTG effects and every legacy
Toolbar application are not covered.

Application results
-------------------
Functional in emulators: IBrowse 3.0a (page rendering, URL editing, links,
HTTP downloads, tab handling, bookmarks, menu Quit, settings pages),
YAM 2.x, Voyager 3.5.2, Odyssey Web Browser, AmiPG, XSysInfo. All 12
built-in preferences pages display images; MCP pages appear once; Prefs
Test and Cancel return successfully. Native list-tree glyphs and some
settings layouts differ from MUI. Newstring.mcc clipboard behaviour matches
MUI 3.9.

IBrowse 3.0a runs with Newstring 16.18 and with the String-derived 21.26
supplied with MUI 5. The latter requires muimaster.library version 20; a
MUI5_TEST=0 build shows "Couldn't create CL_URLString" with it. Check both
MUI:Libs/mui and the browser's own mui drawer.

LumiZip 1.0.1 and LumiWeather 1.4.524 start and quit cleanly on AmigaOS
3.2.3 with networking off. A shutdown freeze reported on OS 3.9/AfA OS has
not been reproduced.

Not covered by tests so far: settings edits, form handling, further
applications, 68000 machines with OS 3.1 on real hardware, CyberGraphX
truecolor rendering.

Settings, Snapshot, Popup and Iconify title-bar buttons are implemented;
iconify/restore and palette ownership work. With the default smart
refresh the main windows of IBrowse, YAM and Voyager restore completely
when uncovered. After a resize, YAM's message list area differs from MUI
3.9 in both refresh modes.

Stress tests: 1,000 control and 1,000 queued-call lifetimes after warmup
complete with flat free memory and largest blocks and no discarded-method
callbacks. 100 window cycles complete with balanced setup/cleanup and
stable memory and system colours. One window test ended 112 bytes of fast
RAM below its initial sample, so a window leak is not ruled out. These
results are not a general leak-free claim.

Runtime workload
----------------
From an Amiga Shell, after installing and rebooting:

    Tests/compatcheck muimaster.library 100 >RAM:openmui-contracts.txt
    Tests/compatcheck muimaster.library 100 gui >RAM:openmui-gui.txt

The executable requests a 64 KiB stack. The optional gui mode repeatedly
opens and disposes a window, including group relayout. Run on a test
installation: these workloads exercise code which still needs GUI review.

The output separates deterministic 'contract' rows from 'metric' and
'memory' rows. It records notification values, numeric scaling, sorted
insertion, comparison calls, elapsed DOS ticks, free Chip/Fast RAM and
largest blocks. Numeric cases use observed MUI 3.8/3.9 results for shifted,
reversed and single-value ranges.
Two lifecycle iterations warm caches before the repeated-run baseline.
Library closure does not force expunge; the 'closed' sample may retain
idle classes or other OS resources. No automatic memory threshold is
claimed: other tasks, caches and fragmentation affect the measurements.

Repeat with 10, 100 and 1000 iterations and two simultaneous clients.
Record CPU/clock, OS, screen depth, RAM, compiler flags and exact binaries.
Compare trends, not a single free-memory sample. Existing opentest and
Prefs remain the interactive checks for keyboard focus, layout, clipboard,
MCP pages, Use/Save/Cancel, ARexx and Workbench integration.

Original SDK comparison
-----------------------
On the build host, point make at an extracted original SDK:

    make check-sdk MUI38_SDK=/path/to/MUI/Developer
    make check-sdk MUI_SDK=/path/to/SDK/MUI MUI_SDK_VERSION=3.9

This compiles the same client using the original public definitions and
regenerates only the obsolete GCC inline assembly from that SDK's FD and
prototypes. It leaves the original SDK untouched and does not redistribute
it. The outputs are build/muimaster/sdk-check/compatcheck-mui38 and
compatcheck-mui39, respectively. The MUI 5 SDK also works as the 3.9
SDK.

It then compares the value of every identifier that the SDK's
libraries/mui.h and mui/*_mcc.h define and OpenMUI also defines, and
fails if any differ. Image and frame counts differ by design, so they
are listed but not counted as errors. tools/check_sdk_ids.py --missing
lists the SDK identifiers that OpenMUI does not define.

Run that binary in separate MUI and OpenMUI installations, then compare:

    python3 tools/compare_compat.py mui-contracts.txt openmui-contracts.txt

Matching contract output is one narrow result. Inspect timing/memory
separately and exercise real application/MCC versions before making broad
compatibility claims. Hosted GitHub Actions and physical hardware
still require their own runs.
